+# Contributing
+
+Thank you for improving this awesome list.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+- Every submission must include a public `github.com` repository URL.
+- No proprietary SaaS-only tools.
+- No products without a public GitHub repository.
+- Include stars, primary language, license, and last commit date from GitHub.
+- Explain why the project belongs in this list and which category it fits.
+- If a repository is archived, inactive, or has a non-standard license, note that explicitly.
+
+## Pull Request Format
+
+Please add new tools using this structure:
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/username/repo)
+> **Description:** Short summary of what the tool does, what makes it relevant, and GitHub metadata including stars, language, license, and last commit date.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/username/repo](https://github.com/username/repo)
+- **Stars:** 1234 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-29
+- **Category:** tag1, tag2
+- **Best for:** specific use case
+```
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Prefer repositories with clear documentation and active maintenance.
+- Avoid duplicate entries across categories unless there is a strong reason.
+- Do not add filler projects just to increase count.
+- If a category has too few strong repos, document the gap instead of padding it.
+
+## Verification Checklist
+
+Before opening a pull request, verify:
+
+- The repository URL works and points to `github.com`.
+- The repository is public.
+- The license is visible on GitHub.
+- The project is relevant to AI agents or agent infrastructure.
+- The description is accurate and not copied verbatim in long form from the source README.
