+---
+title: "best-open-source-ai-agents-2026"
+description: "Discover the best open source AI agents for 2026: coding agents, browser agents, self-hosted platforms, and frameworks with GitHub links."
+icon: 📋
+category: Developer Tools
+---
+
+# Best Open Source AI Agents 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-open-source-ai-agents-2026?style=social)
+
+> Best open source AI agents 2026 is a hand-curated list of GitHub-hosted agent frameworks, coding agents, browser agents, and self-hosted AI platforms. Every entry links to a public `github.com` repository, includes GitHub metadata, and is selected for practical use in real agentic workflows rather than hype alone.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- `open-swe`, `goose`, `plandex`, and `qwen-code` are the strongest coding-agent picks if you want repositories focused on software tasks.
+- `TEN-framework`, `google/adk-python`, `google/adk-go`, and `voltagent` stand out if you need an extensible framework or SDK for building agents yourself.
+- `UI-TARS-desktop`, `nanobrowser`, `browser-agent`, and `steel-browser` are useful for browser automation and computer-use workflows.
+- `LibreChat`, `Khoj`, `DB-GPT`, and `IntentKit` are good self-hosted options when you need agents behind your own infrastructure.
+- If a niche subcategory did not have at least five strong public GitHub repos, it was folded into a broader section instead of being padded with weak or unclear projects.
+
+## Why This List
+The best open source AI agents 2026 are increasingly GitHub-native projects that developers can inspect, fork, self-host, and adapt to their own stack. That matters for security reviews, cost control, long-term maintenance, and avoiding lock-in to closed SaaS workflows.
+
+This list intentionally excludes proprietary SaaS-only tools and non-GitHub products. Every item here points to a public GitHub repository, and each description highlights practical fit, license posture, and GitHub activity so you can compare options quickly.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Agent Frameworks And SDKs
+
+#### [TEN Framework](https://github.com/TEN-framework/ten-framework)
+> **Description:** TEN is positioned in its README as an open-source framework for conversational voice AI agents, with agent examples, self-hosting paths, and a broader ecosystem around voice-first experiences. It is a strong fit when you need multi-component agent systems with real-time or speech-oriented workflows instead of a simple text chatbot. In the GitHub research snapshot it had 10,376 stars, used Python as the primary language, and showed an `Other` license classification. GitHub commit history shows the latest commit on March 10, 2026, which signals ongoing maintenance.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/TEN-framework/ten-framework](https://github.com/TEN-framework/ten-framework)
+- **Stars:** 10,376 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Other
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-10
+- **Category:** framework, voice-agent
+- **Best for:** Building conversational and voice-centric agent systems
+
+---
+
+#### [Agent Development Kit for Python](https://github.com/google/adk-python)
+> **Description:** Google’s ADK README describes a code-first Python framework for building, evaluating, and deploying sophisticated AI agents with flexibility and control. The project emphasizes modular multi-agent composition, deployment options, and model-agnostic design even though it is optimized for Gemini. That makes it one of the clearest framework choices for teams who want structure without giving up implementation freedom. In the research snapshot it had 18,653 stars, Python as the primary language, Apache-2.0 licensing, and GitHub commit history confirms a latest commit date of March 18, 2026.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/google/adk-python](https://github.com/google/adk-python)
+- **Stars:** 18,653 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-18
+- **Category:** framework, sdk
+- **Best for:** Python teams building structured production agent workflows
+
+---
+
+#### [Agent Development Kit for Go](https://github.com/google/adk-go)
+> **Description:** The Go ADK repository is the same code-first idea as the Python toolkit, but aimed at Go teams that want typed services, agent orchestration, and deployment control in a Go-first stack. The README frames it as an open-source toolkit for building, evaluating, and deploying sophisticated AI agents, which is useful if your infra and backend services already live in Go. The research snapshot lists 7,277 stars, Go as the primary language, and Apache-2.0 licensing. GitHub commit history shows the latest commit landed on March 3, 2026.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/google/adk-go](https://github.com/google/adk-go)
+- **Stars:** 7,277 ⭐
+- **Language:** Go
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-03
+- **Category:** framework, sdk
+- **Best for:** Go-native agent backends and internal developer platforms
+
+---
+
+#### [VoltAgent](https://github.com/VoltAgent/voltagent)
+> **Description:** VoltAgent presents itself as an AI agent engineering platform built on an open source TypeScript AI agent framework. The project is useful for teams that want a TypeScript-first foundation for agent orchestration, developer ergonomics, and productized agent workflows rather than a bare-bones starter kit. It fits well in full-stack JavaScript shops that want to keep agent logic close to the rest of the application code. In the provided GitHub research it had 7,117 stars, TypeScript as the primary language, an MIT license, and GitHub commit history shows a latest commit on March 21, 2026.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/VoltAgent/voltagent](https://github.com/VoltAgent/voltagent)
+- **Stars:** 7,117 ⭐
+- **Language:** TypeScript
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-21
+- **Category:** framework, typescript-agent
+- **Best for:** TypeScript-native agent engineering and product teams
+
+---
+
+#### [Agentic](https://github.com/transitive-bullshit/agentic)
+> **Description:** Agentic is an API-to-MCP oriented project that explored turning existing APIs into monetizable or reusable MCP-compatible tooling. Even though the repository was archived in February 2026, it remains useful as an open GitHub reference for API-centric agent architectures, MCP compatibility ideas, and TypeScript implementations of agent infrastructure patterns. In the research snapshot it had 18,130 stars, TypeScript as the primary language, and GitHub classified the license as `Other`. GitHub commit history shows the latest commit on July 21, 2025, so it should be treated as a reference project rather than a fast-moving core dependency.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/transitive-bullshit/agentic](https://github.com/transitive-bullshit/agentic)
+- **Stars:** 18,130 ⭐
+- **Language:** TypeScript
+- **License:** Other
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-07-21
+- **Category:** framework, mcp
+- **Best for:** Studying API-to-MCP patterns and archived TypeScript agent infrastructure
+
+---
+
+### Coding Agents And Developer Automation
+
+#### [Open SWE](https://github.com/langchain-ai/open-swe)
+> **Description:** Open SWE’s README is unusually explicit about what it is: an open-source framework for building an internal coding agent with cloud sandboxes, Slack and Linear invocation, subagent orchestration, and automatic pull request creation. That makes it a practical choice for engineering teams building their own internal code assistant instead of relying on a closed hosted product. In the research snapshot it had 8,805 stars, used Python as the primary language, and was MIT licensed. GitHub commit history shows the latest commit on March 25, 2026, which is strong recent activity for a coding-agent project.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/langchain-ai/open-swe](https://github.com/langchain-ai/open-swe)
+- **Stars:** 8,805 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-25
+- **Category:** coding-agent, developer-tools
+- **Best for:** Internal engineering bots, PR automation, and sandboxed coding workflows
+
+---
+
+#### [Goose](https://github.com/block/goose)
+> **Description:** Goose is presented as an open source, extensible AI agent that goes beyond code suggestions by installing, executing, editing, and testing with any LLM. That framing matters because it positions Goose as an active developer workflow agent, not just a chat shell around code completion. It is a strong option for teams that value CLI workflows and extensibility. In the supplied research it had 33,737 stars, Rust as the primary language, and Apache-2.0 licensing. GitHub commit history shows the latest commit on March 29, 2026, making it one of the freshest entries in this list.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/block/goose](https://github.com/block/goose)
+- **Stars:** 33,737 ⭐
+- **Language:** Rust
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-29
+- **Category:** coding-agent, cli
+- **Best for:** Terminal-first coding automation with strong extensibility
+
+---
+
+#### [Plandex](https://github.com/plandex-ai/plandex)
+> **Description:** Plandex is an open source AI coding agent designed for large projects and real-world tasks, which is a useful distinction from toy repositories optimized for demos only. Its pitch is centered on navigating larger codebases and sustained development sessions, so it fits teams working in monorepos or long-lived engineering work. In the GitHub research snapshot it had 15,166 stars, Go as the primary language, and an MIT license. GitHub commit history shows the latest commit on October 3, 2025, so the repository remains notable but is less recently updated than the fastest-moving options above.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/plandex-ai/plandex](https://github.com/plandex-ai/plandex)
+- **Stars:** 15,166 ⭐
+- **Language:** Go
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-10-03
+- **Category:** coding-agent, large-codebase
+- **Best for:** Larger repositories and longer-running code tasks
+
+---
